In this lesson, we'll look at two ways to move shapes around on a coordinate plane: reflections and rotations. First up, reflections. Imagine you have a point on a graph and you flip it across one of the axes. If you flip across the x-axis, the point moves up or down like a mirror, so its left-right position stays the same, but its up-down position swaps. If you flip across the y-axis, it's the opposite — the up-down position stays, and the left-right swaps. Next, rotations. Here, you spin the shape around the origin, which is the point right at zero, zero. A 90 degree spin sends each point to a new spot depending on whether you turn clockwise or counter-clockwise, and the two directions land in completely different places. A 180 degree spin is simpler, since turning either way gives the same result. One key thing to remember is orientation — translations keep text reading the same, reflections make it look like a mirror image, and rotations tilt it by however many degrees you turn. Now let's see it in action.